Claim boundaries, stated so they travel with every installed copy:

1. limen rules on measurement stability only. No output of this package is a
   statement about which model is better; a SIGN-UNSTABLE ruling means "this
   comparison is not supported by its own measurement", never "the other model
   wins".

2. Results limen produces over the Spaghetti-Architect four-model ladder
   archives are statements about the verdict stability of those committed
   archives as re-analysed here — instruments over the same population, not
   claims about, or corrections to, any table published from that ladder
   elsewhere.

3. Validity is scoped to deterministic exact-match grading. limen says nothing
   about judge-scored, rubric-scored or preference-scored tasks.

The measurement definitions (TARa@N, swap/rank-flip rate, decision-consistency
style constancy, the exclude-then-re-rank step) are prior art, several of them
decades old, and are cited under their original names in the documentation.
The contribution is the installable instrument, not a phenomenon.
